00:17:58,992 --> 00:18:06,672
我们长期以来一直在推测它具体如何影响开源和开
发，现在终于看到了一些具体的数据，真是太好了。
We've been speculating for a long time on exactly how it's been affecting open source and development,
but it's nice to see some concrete numbers finally.

184
00:18:06,926 --> 00:18:08,992
它让我们更清晰地了解了现状。
It gives us a clearer picture of what's actually happening.

185
00:18:09,102 --> 00:18:17,220
它还展示了 AI 使用情况的两面，因为开发者明确表示，根据任务的不同，他们节省了一些时间。
And it also shows us both sides of the AI use story, as clearly developers report saving some time, depending on the task.

186
00:18:17,312 --> 00:18:24,192
但维护者，尤其是那些参与大型项目的人，正经历着倦怠和巨大的审查工作量。
But maintainers, especially the ones working in large projects, are experiencing burnout and huge review workloads.

187
00:18:24,352 --> 00:18:27,312
而这全是因为提交了低质量的代码。
And that's all due to low quality code being submitted.

188
00:18:27,472 --> 00:18:33,312
这意味着 AI 可以提高人们的生产力，但它也可能带来新的挑战，包括分散注意力。
Meaning AI can make people more productive, but it can also create new challenges, including distraction.

189
00:18:33,552 --> 00:18:38,308
这是对 AI 如何重塑开源开发的一次非常必要的审视。
This is a much needed look at how AI has been reshaping open source dev.

190
00:18:39,278 --> 00:18:41,872
甚至连 Linus Torvalds 本人也在使用 AI。
As even Linus Torvalds himself is using AI.

191
00:18:42,030 --> 00:18:52,992
作为一名元工程师，基里尔在 Linus Torvalds 利用 AI 作为工具进行初步探索并得出一些发现
后，帮助 Linus 提交了一个补丁，从而导致了这次重构。
As a meta engineer, Kirill here helped Linus submit a patch after initial idea and some findings from Linus Torvalds while he was
using AI as a tool to probe around a little bit, which led to this rework.

192
00:18:53,072 --> 00:18:58,672
这是 Linus 在 RFC 下提出的想法，经过重构后由这位元工程师提交。
This is Linus's idea under a request for comment that got reworked, submitted by this meta engineer.

193
00:18:58,832 --> 00:19:04,192
这样的 folio 只有在 RCU 宽限期之后才会被释放。
A folio that such a reader may copy from is freed only after RCU grace period.

194
00:19:04,366 --> 00:19:14,032
这里的原始想法是，通过允许某些读取和页缓存，而无
需反复获取或释放对相同内存页的引用，从而提高性能。
The original idea here was to improve performance by allowing certain reads and page
cache without repeatedly taking on or releasing references to the same memory pages.

195
00:19:14,222 --> 00:19:26,512
这些也被称为 folios，就在几天前，Linus 实际上发布了一个包含八个补丁的系列，
他在其中提到 Claude Code 在他的指导下帮助他生成代码、进行测试并创建评审笔记。
Those are also known as folios, in which just a few days ago, Linus actually published an eight patch series in which he credited
Claude Code on helping him produce the code and test while creating review notes, all under his direction.

196
00:19:26,702 --> 00:19:29,668
然后他向内核邮件列表提交了这一变更建议。
Then he proposed the change to the kernel mailing list.

197
00:19:29,806 --> 00:19:38,032
接着就在昨天，基里尔提交了我们现在看到的这个扩展后的 18 个补丁系列，用来实现这一改进。
And then just yesterday, Kirill here submitted the expanded 18-patch series
that we can see before us right here to actually make the improvement.

198
00:19:38,222 --> 00:19:50,192
正如你看到的，Linus 作为一名经验丰富的开发者和 Linux 的创始人，
也在使用 AI 作为工具来加速复杂的工程工作，并且是以工具本该有的方式在利用它。
As you can see, Linus, a highly experienced dev and the founder of Linux, is also using AI as a tool to accelerate difficult
engineering work and using it in a way that it was intended to be used as a tool.

199
00:19:50,382 --> 00:19:56,272
它甚至让另一位真正理解 AI 辅助代码的维护者能够对其进行评审并基于此开展工作。
It even allowed another maintainer who actually understood the AI assisted code to review it and work off of it.

200
00:19:56,432 --> 00:20:00,912
我们从中得到的结论是，我们得到了性能更好的代码，这太棒了。
What we get from this is the fact that we get more performant code, which is awesome.

201
00:20:01,166 --> 00:20:06,192
我们很希望看到它出现在 Linux 内核中，并且希望我们能像这次一样，继续负责任地使用它。
We'd love to see it in the Linux kernel, and hopefully we can keep using it responsibly, just like in this case.

202
00:20:06,414 --> 00:20:08,592
但这仍然留下了一些重要的问题未解决。
But it still leaves some important questions unanswered.

203
00:20:08,752 --> 00:20:11,472
AI 真的节省了开源项目的时间吗？
Does AI actually save open source time?

204
00:20:11,630 --> 00:20:14,592
还是说它只是把工作从贡献者转移到了维护者身上？
Or does it shift work from contributors to maintainers?

205
00:20:14,798 --> 00:20:17,540
谁才是这些生产力提升的真正受益者？
Who's actually benefiting from these productivity gains?

206
00:20:17,712 --> 00:20:20,032
还是说我们实际上在付出代价？
And are we just actually paying a price?

207
00:20:20,206 --> 00:20:22,432
除非我们经历几年这样的情况，否则我们无法知道。
We won't know until we've had years of this.

208
00:20:22,592 --> 00:20:25,952
这会让维护者倦怠变得更严重吗？
Is this going to make maintainer burnout even worse?

209
00:20:26,222 --> 00:20:28,192
看起来确实正朝着那个方向发展。
It does seem like it's going in that direction.

210
00:20:28,352 --> 00:20:34,788
如果维护者最终需要依靠 AI 才能跟上 AI 生成的贡献，那会发生什么？
And what happens if maintainers end up needing AI just to keep up with the AI generated contributions?

211
00:20:35,054 --> 00:20:36,100
这还有意义吗？
Does it make any sense?

212
00:20:36,272 --> 00:20:39,872
因为如果这种情况长期存在，可维护性会变成什么样？
Because what happens to maintainability if that's the case long term?

213
00:20:40,238 --> 00:20:43,392
在我们回答这些问题的过程中，未来的几年可能会走上一段艰难的道路。
It's going to be a rough road over the coming years as we answer some of these questions.

214
